    <string name="factor_desc_cover">High cloud works best: thin enough to be lit from below. Thick low cloud blocks the light; 20–90%% cover is ideal.</string>
    <string name="factor_desc_base">A higher base gives a wider, more varied glow; a low base confines it to orange-red and short duration.</string>
    <string name="factor_desc_heq">Aerosols lift the effective ground. The larger it is, the lower the effective cloud base and the harder the glow forms.</string>
    <string name="factor_desc_heff">Cloud base minus optical equivalent ground — the real input to the geometry test.</string>
    <string name="factor_desc_dedge">Cloud-edge distance sampled every 25 km along the sun azimuth. It must stay inside 2√(2Rh) for light to reach under the deck.</string>
    <string name="factor_desc_block">Low cloud sits under the light path. High low-cloud cover is the most common reason a glow fails.</string>
    <string name="factor_desc_aod">More aerosol means hazier sky and weaker glow; very little gives the purest colour.</string>
    <string name="factor_desc_vis">How clear the lower atmosphere is. Below 5 km, haze or fog blocks the low-angle glow.</string>
    <string name="factor_desc_rh">Moderate humidity helps cloud and scattering; above 90%% brings fog, too low may mean no cloud.</string>
    <string name="factor_desc_stab">Steady pressure means a slow-moving system and predictable cloud edge; rapid change makes the forecast less certain.</string>
    <string name="factor_desc_wind">Wind component along the sun azimuth. Below 15 m/s the edge stays put; above 30 m/s it shifts a lot.</string>
    <string name="factor_desc_cape">Strong convection builds local cumulus that may block the path; anvil cloud can also make a fine sunset.</string>
    <string name="factor_desc_consist">How much the three models disagree for the same hour. Smaller spread means you can plan on it.</string>
